¶
'''theoretically''' (thē-ọ̄-ret′i-kal-i), ''adv.'' In a theoretic manner; in or by theory; from a theoretical point of view; speculatively: opposed to ''practically.''

'''theoretician''' (thē″ọ̄-re-tish′a̤n), ''n.'' [< ''theoretic'' + ''-ian.''] A theorist; a theorizer; one who is expert in the theory of a science or art.

'''theoretics''' (thē-ọ̄-ret′iks), ''n.'' [Pl. of ''theoretic'' (see ''-ics'').] The speculative parts of a science.

{{smaller block|With our Lord himself and his apostles, as represented to us in the New Testament, morals come before contemplation, ethics before ''theoretics.''
{{right|''H. B. Wilson.''}}}}

'''theoric<sup>1</sup>†''' (thē′ọ̄-rik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [I. ''a.'' = F. ''théorique'' = Sp. ''teórico'' = Pg. ''theorico'' = It. ''teorico,'' < ML. ''theoricus,'' < Gr. θεωρικός, of or pertaining to theory, < θεωρία, theory: see ''theory.'' II. ''n.'' Also ''theorick, theorique,'' < ME. ''theorik, theorike,'' < OF. ''theorique,'' F. ''théorique'' = Sp. ''teorica'' = Pg. ''theorica'' = It. ''teorica,'' < ML. ''theorica'' (sc. ''ars''), < Gr. θεωρικός, of or pertaining to theory: see I.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Making deductions from theory, especially from imperfect theory; theorizing. Also ''theorical.''

{{smaller block|Your courtier ''theoric'' is he that hath arrived to his farthest, and doth now know the court rather by speculation than practice.
{{right|''B. Jonson,'' Cynthia's Revels, ii. 1.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|A man but young,<br>Yet old in judgment; ''theoric'' and practic<br>In all humanity.
{{right|''Massinger and Field'', Fatal Dowry, ii. 1.}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' 1. Theory; speculation; that which is theoretical.

{{smaller block|The bookish ''theoric'',<br>Wherein the toged consuls can propose<br>As masterly as he; mere prattle, without practice,<br>Is all his soldiership.
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, i. 1. 24.}}}}

{{smaller block|An abstract of the ''theorick'' and practick in the Æsculapian art.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Volpone, ii. 1.}}}}

2. A treatise or part of a treatise containing scientific explanation of phenomena.

{{smaller block|The 4 partie shal ben a ''theorik'' to declare the moevynge of the celestial bodies with the causes.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Astrolabe, Prol.}}}}

'''theoric<sup>2</sup>''' (thẹ̄-or′ik), ''a.'' [< Gr. θεωρικός, of or pertaining to public spectacles, τὰ θεωρικά, or τὸ θεωρικόν, the theoric fund (< θεωρία, a viewing: see ''theory''. Cf. ''theoric''<sup>1</sup>).] Of or pertaining to public spectacles, etc.—{{smaller|'''Theoric fund''', in ''Athenian antiq.'', same as ''theoricon''.}}

'''theorical†''' (thẹ̄-or′i-ka̤l), ''a.'' [< ''theoric''<sup>1</sup> + ''-al''.] Same as ''theoric''<sup>1</sup>.

{{smaller block|I am sure wisdom hath perfected natural disposition in you, and given you not only an excellent ''theorical'' discourse, but an actual reducing of those things into practice which are better than you shall find here.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, III., p. xli.}}}}

'''theorically†''' (thẹ̄-or′i-ka̤l-i), ''adv.'' Theoretically; speculatively.

{{smaller block|He is very musicall, both ''theorically'' and practically, and he had a sweet voyce.
{{right|''Aubrey'', Lives (William Holder).}}}}
